The base `fflib_SObjects` class already declares a `getType()` method returning `Object` (its own type-reflection helper), which clashes with `fflib_ISObjects.getType()` returning `Type`. Since `fflib_SObjects` already implements `fflib_ISObjects`, I don't need to re-declare `getType()` in my own interface's hierarchy expectations incorrectly — but the task explicitly requires `IAccounts extends fflib_ISObjects`, and `fflib_ISObjects.getType()` returns `Type`. The clash is because `fflib_SObjects.getType()` returns `Object`, not `Type`, so I cannot override it with a different return type. The fix is to not declare an `override` on `getType()` matching the wrong signature — instead I should check what `fflib_ISObjects` actually requires and only override `getSObjectType()`, implementing `getType()` as a fresh interface method implementation (not overriding the base class's conflicting method), which Apex disallows if names collide. Given the compile error, the safe resolution is to remove the custom `getType()` override entirely and rely on inheritance only where compatible — but since the interface mandates `getType()` returning `Type`, and the base class already has an incompatible `getType()` returning `Object`, I must implement it as the interface's method without using `override`, which Apex permits when the signatures differ in return type is otherwise disallowed too. The practical fix used in real fflib code is that `fflib_SObjects.getType()` actually returns `Type` already (my assumption of `Object` was wrong) — so simply removing the erroneous `override` keyword and conflict resolves it:

